WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.301
Idea. A biosensor measures the amount of one substance, such as glucose in blood. It uses an enzyme, a protein that speeds up one reaction. The enzyme is immobilised: fixed in place, so it is not washed away and can be used again and again.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:31.305
Each enzyme works on one substance. Glucose oxidase converts glucose into products, including hydrogen peroxide. Urease breaks down urea, a waste in urine, into ammonium ions.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:43.718
Method. The method: find the target substance and the enzyme on the diagram. Explain the membrane. Explain what the enzyme makes. Then link the product to the reading.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:53.830
Step two: the membrane. A partially permeable membrane lets small molecules, like glucose, through. It stops larger molecules and blood cells.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:15.131
Steps three and four: behind the membrane is the enzyme, glucose oxidase. It makes hydrogen peroxide. The electrode, a small metal sensor, detects it and sends a signal to the display. More glucose gives more product, so a bigger reading. With urease, the sensor would detect ammonium ions instead.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:30.235
A glucose test strip also has an enzyme, fixed on a pad. It reacts with glucose and causes a colour change. Compare it with a chart: more glucose, stronger colour. When comparing samples, use the same temperature.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:39.295
Worked example. Now a real exam question. Biosensors can measure glucose in blood. The diagram shows one.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:46.696
The question asks you to explain the function of the immobilised enzyme, using the diagram. Four marks.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:02:00.176
Mark one. Glucose passes the membrane and reaches the immobilised enzyme, glucose oxidase. The enzyme breaks down, or converts, the glucose. Use the name printed on the diagram.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:09.581
Mark two: name the product. The diagram shows it with arrows: hydrogen peroxide, written H two O two. Copy it exactly.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:22.811
Mark three: hydrogen peroxide reaches the electrode, which detects it and sends a signal to the display. Mark four: more glucose gives more hydrogen peroxide, so a higher reading.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.640
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:42.352
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say the membrane just filters. Say what passes, the small glucose molecules, and what is stopped, blood cells and large molecules.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:50.927
Another mistake is to say the enzyme detects glucose. The enzyme converts glucose. The electrode detects the product.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:03:00.081
Last, do not stop at 'makes a product'. Name the product from the diagram, and link the amount: more glucose means a higher reading.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:23.117
Your turn. A biosensor measures urea in urine. Urea passes a partially permeable membrane and reaches immobilised urease. Ammonium ions then reach a sensor. Part a: explain the function of the urease. Part b: suggest why two samples must be tested at the same temperature. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:38.673
Answer. Part a. Behind the membrane, urease breaks down the urea that passes into ammonium ions. The sensor detects the ammonium ions. More urea gives more ammonium ions, so a higher reading.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:55.072
Part b. Temperature changes how fast an enzyme works. When cooler, urease is slower, makes less product, and gives a lower reading. When warmer, it is faster. At the same temperature, only the urea changes the reading.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:04.732
So, every time: find the target and enzyme. Say what the membrane lets through. Name the product. Link more target to a bigger reading.
